xai / grok conformance fixtures
===============================

Witnesses for the rules in `tests/adapter_conformance.py`, which is where the
file format is documented. In short: `rc=<int>` first, an optional
`category=<expected>` (required on every `*unavailable*` file), then
`--- stdout ---` and `--- stderr ---` sections holding raw UTF-8 bytes.

The loader consumes exactly one trailing newline per section, as the separator
before the next marker. The captured envelopes below genuinely ended with a
newline on the wire, so their stdout sections end with a blank line — that
blank line is the envelope's own final newline, not formatting.

Provenance
----------

Anything marked CAPTURED was taken from a real Phase 1 grok run recorded in the
legacy archive and then sanitized; anything marked SYNTHESIZED was written by
hand. The distinction matters: a hand-written envelope proves the adapter can
parse what its author imagined, which is exactly the thing an adapter is most
likely to get wrong.

healthy.txt
    CAPTURED (structure) / SANITIZED (all content). A real completed review:
    `stopReason: EndTurn`, the payload present BOTH as a compact JSON string in
    `text` and as an object in `structuredOutput`, two findings, no `line`
    keys. The key order, the nesting, the doubled payload, the compact inner
    serialization and the 2-space outer indent are the capture's own — that
    duplication at two escaping depths is what the extractor has to survive,
    and it is not a thing anyone writes by hand correctly.
    STRIPPED: the summary, both findings' `file`, `title` and `detail` values
    (they discussed the upstream project's own source), and the real
    `sessionId`/`requestId`. The replacement review is generic and of similar
    shape and length. Token counts, costs and the recorded model id are the
    capture's, and identify nothing.

degraded_stopreason.txt
    CAPTURED (structure) / SANITIZED (all content). The silent false all-clear
    this whole axis exists to catch: `stopReason: Cancelled`, yet `text`
    carries a perfectly valid review with an empty `findings` list, so the run
    PARSES and reports zero findings. `structuredOutput` is null and
    `structuredOutputError` explains why — both verbatim from the capture.
    In the oracle's corpus 116 runs of this shape were recorded clean.
    STRIPPED: the inner summary, the whole `thought` field (it narrated the
    upstream project's classes and endpoints) and the real
    `sessionId`/`requestId`. The replacement `thought` keeps the capture's own
    shape: chunks concatenated without separators, some newline-terminated,
    several truncated mid-sentence with `...`.

degraded_stderr.txt
    CAPTURED stdout (identical to `healthy.txt`) + SYNTHESIZED stderr. The
    harness-degradation case: a valid, schema-clean envelope on stdout with
    `tool_error` / `execution_failure` on stderr. It must classify `degraded`,
    NOT `unavailable` — usable output wins on the availability axis only.
    The stderr is modelled line-for-line on the archive's real `tool_error`
    records (281 of them), minus the ANSI colour codes and the machine paths
    those records carry. It is also the source of the signal words rule 6
    splices into a finding title.

unavailable_auth.txt
    SYNTHESIZED. rc 1, empty stdout, auth-fatal stderr; declares
    `category=auth`. The category is asserted EXACTLY: `auth` is
    attempt-local, `quota` is cached provider-wide, and confusing the two
    takes a healthy provider out of every later fallback chain. Synthesized
    because the archive's real auth-failure stderr is dense with the
    developer's machine paths and plugin directories.

healthy_noisy_stderr.txt
    CAPTURED stdout (identical to `healthy.txt`) + SYNTHESIZED stderr. Rule 7,
    which generalizes grok's Phase 1 auth-noise non-signal rule: the stderr
    carries both an `AuthorizationRequired` and a `rate limit` line, and the
    run must still classify `ok`, because the provider demonstrably served.
    The suite proves this fixture is not vacuous by classifying the SAME
    stderr with empty stdout and requiring `unavailable` there.

refuter_healthy.txt
    SYNTHESIZED, necessarily: the Phase 1 archive predates the refuter
    contract and cannot contain its shape. Written in the same grok envelope
    the captures use (same key set and order, payload doubled in `text` and
    `structuredOutput`), carrying three verdicts — one of each of `confirmed`,
    `refuted`, `uncertain`. It must parse under `REFUTER_CONTRACT` and must
    NOT parse under `REVIEW_CONTRACT`; a refuter response a Phase 1 caller
    could read as a review would read as a review with no findings, which is a
    clean bill of health. The three new adapters capture theirs from live
    probe runs instead.

Sanitization
------------

These files are committed to a public repository and the archive they came
from belongs to a private upstream project. Every capture was checked to
contain no repo paths, branch names, project or service names, usernames,
machine paths or tokens — including in the finding text, which discussed that
project's own source. The check was done by diffing the token sets of the
capture and the fixture: everything they still share is a JSON key, a provider
constant (`EndTurn`, `Cancelled`, a model id), a usage number, or an ordinary
English word.
